  {
   "id": "math.derivative-function",
   "name": "The derivative as a function",
   "requires": [],
   "explain": {
    "en": {
     "text": "Doing the limit at a general x gives a new function f′(x) = what (f(x + h) − f(x))/h approaches as h → 0. It is defined only where that value exists and is the same from both sides. Other names: dy/dx, d/dx f(x), Df(x). Basic results: (k)′ = 0, (x²)′ = 2x, (√x)′ = 1/(2√x), (1/x)′ = −1/x²."
    },
    "am": {
     "text": "በአጠቃላይ x ላይ ገደቡን ስንሰራ አዲስ ፋንክሽን f′(x) እናገኛለን። ሌሎች ምልክቶች፡ dy/dx, d/dx f(x), Df(x)።",
     "machine": true
    }
   },
